# copytree ignore — gitignore syntax. Keep the AI context compact by excluding
# what the Go toolchain generates or what carries no semantic value for reading
# the source.
#
# IMPORTANT: cli/ is a git SUBMODULE (its own repo), and copytree does NOT read a
# submodule's .gitignore — only the top repo's .gitignore and this .copytreeignore.
# So everything cli/.gitignore would normally hide (the build output & binary,
# *.test/*.out, coverage, profiles, logs, state.db, and — critically — real .env
# secrets) is NOT filtered unless it is listed HERE. A 31 MB compiled binary under
# bin/ leaked exactly this way. Do not assume .gitignore covers it; repeat it below.

# --- Build output, test/profile artifacts, secrets (NOT covered by cli/.gitignore) ---
# The compiled binary lives here (bin/daintree-assistant, ~31 MB) — the single
# biggest thing that can bloat the export. dist/ is the packaged-build dir.
bin/
dist/
daintree-assistant
*.exe
# Go test / profiling artifacts.
*.test
*.out
*.prof
*.cpu
*.mem
coverage/
coverage.txt
coverage.html
# Logs & local runtime state.
*.log
logs/
state.db
*.local
.tmp/
# Secrets: drop any real env file, keep the committed example.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- CLI Go implementation: keep ONLY the tool/contract-defining packages ---
# The vendored CLI is checked in so this repo can read the CLI's actual
# tool/function definitions and the backend wire contracts (see the backend
# CLAUDE.md). For an LLM export of the *backend* we want exactly those — not the
# ~1.8 MB of cockpit/daemon/runtime Go with no bearing on the API. We KEEP three
# packages and drop the rest by name:
#   internal/backend/ — the Daintree backend wire contracts (client, SSE, tasks)
#   internal/domain/  — pure vocabulary: RiskClass, Tier, ToolResult, records
#   internal/tools/   — the tool families, where every tool's schema is declared
#
# NB: copytree does NOT support the gitignore whitelist idiom (`internal/*` then
# `!internal/backend/`) — that negation drops ALL of internal/ instead of keeping
# the three. So we enumerate the dropped packages explicitly. A new package
# defaults to *included*, which is the safe direction — review the tree after
# adding one. This list subsumes the old per-file exclusions (internal/ui splash
# art / theme / markdown, internal/deps).
internal/agent/
internal/app/
internal/cli/
internal/commands/
internal/config/
internal/daemon/
internal/debuglog/
internal/deps/
internal/e2e/
internal/host/
internal/mcp/
internal/models/
internal/ports/
internal/projectinstructions/
internal/queue/
internal/safety/
internal/storage/
internal/terminal/
internal/ui/

# The test suite dominates the tree (~half of all .go files). The Go toolchain
# names every test file `*_test.go`, so this single pattern catches all of them
# wherever they live — including inside the kept packages above.
*_test.go

# Conventional Go test fixtures — data-only, irrelevant to reading the code.
testdata/

# Vendored dependencies, if ever checked in (`go mod vendor`): third-party
# source we don't author and don't want bloating the context.
vendor/

# Dependency checksums — 12 KiB of opaque hashes with zero semantic value for an
# AI reading the project. go.mod (module path + the dep list) is kept; go.sum is not.
go.sum

# Runtime recipe config — user-generated/project-specific data, not source code.
.daintree/

# Superseded doc — DEEPSEEK.md self-declares "SUPERSEDED: the CLI no longer talks to
# DeepSeek directly" (the backend does now). Worse than dead weight: feeding it to an AI
# actively misleads it about how the model gateway works. Excluded as stale, not for size.
docs/DEEPSEEK.md
